### What is the problem?

Given n pairs of parentheses, generate every string of n opens and n closes that is well-formed. For n = 3 there are exactly 5 valid strings. The brute force generates all 2^(2n) binary strings and filters, but the elegant approach never builds an invalid string.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class GenerateParenthesesBacktrack {
    public static List<String> generate(int n) {
        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        backtrack(result, new StringBuilder(), 0, 0, n);
        return result;
    }

    private static void backtrack(List<String> result, StringBuilder sb, int open, int close, int n) {
        if (sb.length() == 2 * n) {
            result.add(sb.toString());
            return;
        }
        if (open < n) {
            sb.append('(');
            backtrack(result, sb, open + 1, close, n);
            sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);
        }
        if (close < open) {
            sb.append(')');
            backtrack(result, sb, open, close + 1, n);
            sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("n=3: " + generate(3));
    }
}
```

```text
n=3: [((())), (()()), (())(), ()(()), ()()()]
```

### Why do the two placement rules guarantee validity?

The rule `open < n` means you can only place an open if you still have budget. The rule `close < open` means you can only place a close if there is an unmatched open before it. Together, they ensure every prefix has at least as many opens as closes, which is the definition of a valid prefix. Since the string ends with equal counts, it is balanced.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class RuleExplanation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Rule 1: open < n  -> budget for opens remains");
        System.out.println("Rule 2: close < open  -> unmatched open exists");
        System.out.println("Together: every prefix has opens >= closes");
        System.out.println("At end: opens == closes == n -> balanced");
    }
}
```

```text
Rule 1: open < n  -> budget for opens remains
Rule 2: close < open  -> unmatched open exists
Together: every prefix has opens >= closes
At end: opens == closes == n -> balanced
```

### How does the backtracking tree look?

At each node, you have at most two branches: place open or place close. The constraints prune invalid branches early. For n=2, only 7 recursive calls produce 2 results, compared to 16 candidates in brute force.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BacktrackTree {
    static int calls = 0;

    static void count(int open, int close, int n, int len) {
        calls++;
        if (len == 2 * n) return;
        if (open < n) count(open + 1, close, n, len + 1);
        if (close < open) count(open, close + 1, n, len + 1);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        count(0, 0, 3, 0);
        System.out.println("Recursive calls for n=3: " + calls);
        System.out.println("Brute force candidates: " + (int) Math.pow(2, 6));
    }
}
```

```text
Recursive calls for n=3: 21
Brute force candidates: 64
```

### What is the Catalan number connection?

The count of valid strings is the nth Catalan number. For n=3 it is 5, for n=4 it is 14, for n=10 it is 16796. This is much smaller than 2^(2n), showing the efficiency of constrained generation. Catalan numbers also count binary trees and polygon triangulations.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CatalanCount {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("n=3: Catalan=5, 2^6=64");
        System.out.println("n=5: Catalan=42, 2^10=1024");
        System.out.println("n=10: Catalan=16796, 2^20=1048576");
    }
}
```

```text
n=3: Catalan=5, 2^6=64
n=5: Catalan=42, 2^10=1024
n=10: Catalan=16796, 2^20=1048576
```

### What are common mistakes?

Generating all strings and filtering is the classic naive mistake. Using string concatenation instead of StringBuilder creates excessive garbage. Forgetting the delete step after recursion corrupts subsequent branches.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BacktrackMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Mistake 1: generate all 2^(2n) and filter -> exponential waste");
        System.out.println("Mistake 2: s + '(' instead of StringBuilder -> GC pressure");
        System.out.println("Mistake 3: forget deleteCharAt -> corrupted builder");
    }
}
```

```text
Mistake 1: generate all 2^(2n) and filter -> exponential waste
Mistake 2: s + '(' instead of StringBuilder -> GC pressure
Mistake 3: forget deleteCharAt -> corrupted builder
```

### What are the edge cases?

For n=0 the result is an empty list or a list with one empty string depending on interpretation. For n=1 there is exactly one result: `"()"`. Large n produces many results due to Catalan growth.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class BacktrackEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("n=0: " + GenerateParenthesesBacktrack.generate(0));
        System.out.println("n=1: " + GenerateParenthesesBacktrack.generate(1));
        System.out.println("n=4 count: " + GenerateParenthesesBacktrack.generate(4).size());
    }
}
```

```text
n=0: []
n=1: [()]
n=4 count: 14
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At the National Bank of India, the approval-rule builder enumerates all valid workflow nesting structures for n levels. Governance reviews every template shape. Constrained generation guarantees each template is valid by construction, saving the review team from examining nonsensical patterns.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class WorkflowTemplates {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int levels = 3;
        List<String> templates = GenerateParenthesesBacktrack.generate(levels);
        System.out.println("Valid workflows for " + levels + " levels:");
        for (String t : templates) {
            System.out.println("  " + t);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Valid workflows for 3 levels:
  ((()))
  (()())
  (())()
  ()(())
  ()()()
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

This constrained backtracking technique, enforcing validity as pruning, generalizes to Sudoku, N-Queens, and valid expression generation. The BFS alternative uses a queue of `(string, open, close)` states. The mathematical connection to Catalan numbers signals maturity. Time complexity is O(4^n / sqrt(n)) which is the Catalan bound.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SeniorBacktrackNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Pattern: validity constraints as pruning");
        System.out.println("Generalizes to: Sudoku, N-Queens, expressions");
        System.out.println("Alternative: BFS queue of (string, open, close)");
        System.out.println("Time: O(4^n / sqrt(n)) = Catalan bound");
    }
}
```

```text
Pattern: validity constraints as pruning
Generalizes to: Sudoku, N-Queens, expressions
Alternative: BFS queue of (string, open, close)
Time: O(4^n / sqrt(n)) = Catalan bound
```
